# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Nothing here is a credential, and `src/env.ts` refuses to
# start on a known placeholder rather than booting with one — a placeholder that boots is a
# placeholder that reaches production.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
BEACON_DATABASE_URL=postgres://beacon:beacon@127.0.0.1:5432/beacon

# Identity's JWKS and issuer, for the operator routes. Token verification is the only thing this
# service asks identity for — and note that identity being unreachable does NOT make Beacon
# unreadable: see BEACON_TOKEN below.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE SCRAPE CREDENTIAL. AD-20 SAID A SCRAPE CONFIG; IT IS A SCRAPE CONFIG AND THIS.
#
# `/metrics` describes the shape of the estate — which services exist, which are down, which
# journey is failing — so it is gated like every other route. Prometheus presents this value in an
# `x-beacon-token` header; `deploy/prometheus/prometheus.yml:90-97` already carries the matching
# `http_headers: files:` block and reads it from a FILE, never from this variable, because a
# credential in an environment variable is a credential in `docker inspect`.
#
# It is also the break-glass credential for the operator routes, which is why it is checked BEFORE
# the identity token: when identity is the thing that has broken, a Beacon that could only be read
# with an identity token would be a Beacon nobody can read.
#
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 32
# At least 24 characters. Leaving a placeholder here is refused at boot, deliberately.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
BEACON_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# --- the estate ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `name=url,name=url`. ONE variable, not one per service.
#
# The service this supersedes carries nineteen — BEACON_NIMBUS_URL, BEACON_PAY_URL, … — each with a
# hardcoded compose-network default, so adding a service to the estate meant editing the monitor's
# source. The probe CATALOGUE is a table (`PUT /v1/probes/:name`); this is only the address book
# the journeys resolve service names against.
#
# A journey whose service is not named here reports `skip` with the reason, and a skip is never
# green — so a partially deployed estate is visibly partial rather than quietly passing.
BEACON_TARGETS=identity=http://127.0.0.1:4001,ledger=http://127.0.0.1:4004,market=http://127.0.0.1:4013,worlds=http://127.0.0.1:4014

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4011
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
BEACON_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- cadence and deadlines ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Probes are cheap and frequent; journeys are expensive and rare. The gap between the two numbers
# is the whole reason there are two mechanisms.
#
# BEACON_PROBE_DEADLINE_MS MUST be below BEACON_PROBE_INTERVAL_MS and boot refuses otherwise: a
# deadline at or above the cadence lets one attempt still be running when the next is due, and the
# schedule stretches past its own period exactly when the network goes bad.
# BEACON_PROBE_INTERVAL_MS=30000
# BEACON_PROBE_DEADLINE_MS=5000
# BEACON_JOURNEY_INTERVAL_MS=300000
# BEACON_JOURNEY_DEADLINE_MS=90000

# One failed probe is a dropped packet. Three in a row is an outage — at a 30-second cadence that
# is 90 seconds to detection, fast enough to matter and slow enough to be true. Two consecutive
# successes close it: hysteresis in BOTH directions, so a flapping target does not produce a
# stream of paired open/close notifications.
# BEACON_FAIL_THRESHOLD=3
# BEACON_RECOVER_THRESHOLD=2

# --- the gate ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How stale a journey result may be and still count as an ANSWER.
#
# THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT NUMBER IN THIS FILE. Past this the gate treats the journey as
# indeterminate and REFUSES — which is what makes "the scheduler died" a refusal rather than a
# permanent green. Defaults to four journey intervals, so one missed run is tolerated and two are
# not.
# BEACON_GATE_FRESHNESS_MS=1200000

# Consecutive green runs a release must show. Three, from 13-operational-model.md:150. One green
# run after a red one is a flake that happened to land the right way up.
# BEACON_GATE_CONSECUTIVE_GREEN=3

# The SLO window, in days. 28 rather than 30: four whole weeks compare like with like.
# BEACON_SLO_WINDOW_DAYS=28

# --- the public status page ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Serve `/api/status/public` without a credential. OFF by default, because "public" is a decision.
#
# The projection is redacted BY CONSTRUCTION — separate types and an allowlist the type system
# enforces — so turning this on cannot leak a target name, an error string or a latency figure.
# See README §The public projection.
# BEACON_PUBLIC_STATUS=false

# --- retention ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Raw checks are the bulk of the data and are worthless once rolled up. `check_rollups` is small
# and is what a 90-day uptime bar is computed from, so it outlives the raw rows by more than a
# year.
#
# 400 IS THE FIGURE AD-20 ACTUALLY DESCRIBES. Prometheus cannot downsample and has one retention
# for all data at one resolution; the 400-day line applies to THIS table, which does implement it.
# See 02-target-architecture.md:501-507.
# BEACON_CHECK_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# BEACON_ROLLUP_RETENTION_DAYS=400
# BEACON_RUN_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# BEACON_INCIDENT_RETENTION_DAYS=400

# --- journeys that need more than an address -----------------------------------------------------

# The origin an SSO hand-off code is minted against, for `identity.handoff`.
#
# A code is bound to ONE origin at mint and matched at redemption. That binding is the whole
# security of the hand-off: without it, an open redirect anywhere in the estate turns a legitimate
# sign-in into a token delivered to somebody else's page. So the journey cannot invent an origin —
# it has to be one identity's own IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS names, and identity answers 403 for
# anything else.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE and means the journey SKIPS with the reason. That is not a pass: the
# gate refuses on a skip. It is also the state of the dev estate today, where
# IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS is not set at all.
# BEACON_HANDOFF_ORIGIN=https://hub.cloudsforge.example

# A long-lived service credential, exchanged for a short-lived scoped token per journey run.
#
# A CREDENTIAL, NEVER AN INJECTED TOKEN. Identity issues service tokens with a 600-second TTL and
# nothing re-mints one, so anything holding an injected token starts answering 401 ten minutes
# after `estate-bootstrap.sh` — the "ten-minute cliff" in deploy/README.md.
# `POST /service-tokens/exchange` turns a credential into a fresh token whenever one is needed,
# which is the only shape that works for a monitor running for weeks.
#
# Unset means the journeys that need a scoped token are ABSENT from the registry, not declared and
# skipping. Beacon cannot hold one today in any case: IDENTITY_SERVICE_TOKEN_GRANTS in
# deploy/compose/docker-compose.estate.yml names thirteen services and `beacon` is not among them,
# so POST /service-credentials answers 500 "no scopes are configured for service 'beacon'".
# BEACON_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL=CHANGE_ME

# --- the browser tier ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OFF by default. A browser is a large optional dependency and a deployment choosing not to ship
# one is making a decision, not suffering an outage — so every browser journey skips with that
# reason rather than failing.
#
# There is nothing for it to point at yet either: doc 22 §8.7 records that
# deploy/compose/docker-compose.estate.yml defines 22 domain services and NO frontend container.
# Browser journeys are declared from the catalogue only when the surfaces they need have addresses
# in BEACON_TARGETS, which is why turning this on alone changes nothing.
#
# Surfaces are addressed through BEACON_TARGETS by their surface key, exactly as services are:
#   BEACON_TARGETS=...,site=https://cloudsforge.example,hub=https://hub.cloudsforge.example
# The address must be the PUBLIC hostname. `http://site:80` would answer, and the page would then
# derive its API hosts from the hostname `site` — failing for a reason that has nothing to do with
# the product, which is worse than a skip.
# BEACON_BROWSER_ENABLED=false

# Absolute path to a Chromium. Empty lets playwright-core find its own, which is what a developer
# machine with an ms-playwright cache wants; an image sets it, because it installs Chromium through
# the package manager rather than through playwright.
# BEACON_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/chromium

# How long ONE page operation may take. Separate from BEACON_JOURNEY_DEADLINE_MS, which covers all
# of them; a browser journey carries its own 120-second deadline because launching Chromium and
# waiting for a SPA to mount is not comparable to a JSON round trip.
# BEACON_BROWSER_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# BEACON_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains "test" —
# the database-backed tests truncate.
